From d55e9658d0309805ca37bad5bf8e41d02c40dc9a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Matthias=20M=C3=BCnch?= <claude@djbase.de>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 20:59:30 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] bebbosshd: harden server-side packet parsing

The server trusted every length a client sends. Bounded reads (bstr/bu32)
now keep each length-prefixed field inside the received packet and drop
the session on any overrun:

- handleUserAuthRequest (reachable before authentication), the KEX_INIT
  algorithm name-lists and KEX_ECDH_INIT key length no longer let a
  crafted length walk the parse pointer past the buffer
- handleChannelData rejects a data length larger than the packet
  (was an out-of-bounds read and an unbounded realloc)
- handleOpenChannel / handleChannelRequest / handleChannelEof /
  handleServiceRequest parse within bounds
- the AEAD tag is compared in constant time

Only affects bebbosshd (sshsession.cpp); the client and the library are
unchanged. Built for m68k (020) and, for verification, on Linux under
AddressSanitizer: a full session (handshake, public-key auth, exec,
200 KB of channel data over loopback) runs with zero memory errors and
the normal path is unaffected. Not adversarially fuzzed; bebbosshd still
must not be run on a network (see SECURITY NOTE).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
